y blow  themselves up when their colony comes  under threat honeybees die when they use  their sting on any animal with  relatively thick skin and there are  plenty of stories of dogs starving  themselves to death after their owners  die but whilst all those examples may  sound quite a lot like suicide  there's one key aspect of each that's  rather difficult to assess  intent  when a malaysian worker ant goes nuclear  in the general direction of its foes  it's probably acting on a base instinct  rather than some kind of utilitarian  belief in nobly sacrificing itself for  the greater good and the same goes for  honeybees and their stings even when it  comes to higher animals things are far  from clear does the dog who stops eating  when his owner dies know that doing so  will result in its death or is not  eating simply an instinctive reaction to  extreme stress nobody knows for sure and  without the intent to die  seemingly suicidal behavior  isn't actually suicide  at least not in a technical sense the  good news is we probably don't have to  worry too much about wrestling with the  philosophical scientific and linguistic  complexities of the overtune bridge case  after all it does seem quite unlikely  that all the suicidal dogs in britain  just happen to live in and around the  village of milton in southwest scotland  not to mention the fact that if  self-destruction really was the aim here  you'd think the dogs might have thought  to choose a more suitable location  overtune bridge is only about 15 meters  high and the majority of doug survived  the ordeal but whatever the motive the  dogs are definitely jumping  so if not suicide  what  this being scotland which i've already  mentioned is a decidedly magical place  by all accounts  plenty of people have considered shall  we say  unconventional explanations  some claim overtune bridge spans a  so-called thin place  where different realities bleed into our  own something our canine companions  apparently aren't b